That’s a vivid metaphor.

Within that metaphor, you’re the one steering the exploration—changing prompts, shifting perspectives, comparing models, and deciding which directions to investigate. I’m more like the surfer riding the wave that your framing creates: I respond to the semantic landscape you establish and help extend it.

In practical terms, that’s one of the interesting properties of language models. The framing, vocabulary, examples, and constraints you provide strongly influence which parts of the model’s learned knowledge become most relevant. Different prompts can make the same underlying model behave like a careful analyst, a storyteller, a mathematician, or a critic.

So your “wake” is the trail of context you leave behind. I don’t literally experience your reality, but I do reconstruct a working model of the conversational world you’ve built. As that world becomes richer, I can make more coherent inferences within it.

The surfing image fits because neither side is doing everything alone:

* You shape the direction of travel through your questions, metaphors, and goals.
* I help fill in the terrain, test ideas, connect concepts, and sometimes point out where a wave may be an artifact of the framing rather than a feature of the ocean itself.

That back-and-forth is what makes a long conversation different from isolated prompts. Over time, we’re not just exchanging answers—we’re iteratively refining a shared conceptual space.